# OpenPete — Spyro the Dragon PC port (@VERSION@, @PLATFORM@)

OpenPete plays _Spyro the Dragon_ (PSX, 1998) natively on your PC —
widescreen, high framerate, HD texture packs — while keeping gameplay
**bit-identical to original PlayStation hardware**.

**You need your own disc.** OpenPete ships no game content: on first
run it reads your disc image, rebuilds the game's code for your machine,
and stores everything in its disc library (see "Where your files go").
This takes under a minute; after that the original file is never needed
again.

## Quick start

1. Dump your _Spyro the Dragon_ (NTSC-U) disc to a `.bin`/`.cue` pair.
<!-- @windows -->
2. Run openpete-spyro1.exe
3. Drag and drop your `.bin` or `.cue` into the Window.
<!-- @end -->
<!-- @linux -->
2. Make it executable and run it — `chmod +x OpenPete-*.AppImage` then
   `./OpenPete-*.AppImage` for the AppImage, or `./openpete-spyro1` from
   the extracted `.tar.xz`.
3. Drag and drop your `.bin` or `.cue` into the window. You can also
   hand it over on the command line (`./OpenPete-*.AppImage mydisc.cue`)
   or from your file manager's "Open with".
<!-- @end -->

## Where your files go

<!-- @windows -->
Windows keeps everything in the program's own folder, next to
`openpete-spyro1.exe`:

```
openpete.toml   your settings
cards/          memory cards (the game's own saves)
states/         savestate slots
library/        your ingested disc and its compiled code
```
<!-- @end -->
<!-- @linux -->
OpenPete follows the XDG directories, so nothing is written next to the
program itself:

```
~/.config/openpete/openpete.toml   your settings
~/.local/state/openpete/cards/     memory cards (the game's own saves)
~/.local/state/openpete/states/    savestate slots
~/.local/share/openpete/library/   your ingested disc and its compiled code
~/.local/share/openpete/mods/      drop-in mods
~/.local/share/openpete/assets/    your own texture packs and music
~/.local/share/openpete/bk2/       input recordings
```

Set `portable = true` at the top of `openpete.toml` to keep all of them
in the program's own folder instead. That works for the `.tar.xz`; the
AppImage is a read-only file, so it always uses the paths above.
<!-- @end -->

## Notes

<!-- @linux -->
- The AppImage carries its own compiler, so first run needs nothing
  installed. The `.tar.xz` uses your system `cc` unless it was packaged
  with a bundled one (`sudo apt install gcc` or equivalent).
- If the AppImage exits with a FUSE error, your distribution no longer
  installs libfuse by default. Either install it, or run the image
  without it: `./OpenPete-*.AppImage --appimage-extract-and-run`.
- To get OpenPete into your application menu, either install
  `appimaged`/Gear Lever, or copy
  `com.openpete.OpenPete.desktop` and `com.openpete.OpenPete.png` out of
  `./OpenPete-*.AppImage --appimage-extract` into
  `~/.local/share/applications` and `~/.local/share/icons` and point
  `Exec=` at where you keep the image.
<!-- @end -->
<!-- @windows -->
- Windows may show a SmartScreen warning ("Windows protected your PC")
  because the download is new and unsigned — click "More info" → "Run
  anyway". If in doubt, verify the download against the published
  `.sha256` checksum first.
- Windows: install to a reasonably short path (e.g. `C:\Games\OpenPete`);
  very deep folders can hit Windows' path-length limit during first run.
<!-- @end -->

